Description: os: -launchd <name>, listen on the sockets of a launchd Sockets entry.
 The XQuartz launch model for any DDX: a launchd job declares a Sockets entry
 (on NextBSD a per-user LaunchAgent with SecureSocketWithKey = DISPLAY, so
 launchd makes a socket in a private mkdtemp(3) directory and exports its
 path as DISPLAY), and launchd starts the server when the first X client
 connects. With -launchd <name> the server collects that entry's descriptors
 with launch_activate_socket(3) (liblaunch, nextbsd/nextbsd-userland#447) and
 adds each with the existing ListenOnOpenFD(), which XQuartz uses for its
 launchd socket. It then opens no listening sockets of its own and writes no
 lock file, like XQuartz.
 .
 A socket skips X authorization (ListenOnOpenFD's noxauth) only when its
 directory belongs to the user who started the server (its real uid: Xorg is
 set-uid root, nextbsd/nextbsd-xlibre#20, so the effective uid is root's) and
 nobody else can enter it, which is what SecureSocketWithKey creates; any
 other launchd socket keeps X authorization and the server logs a warning.
 .
 liblaunch is dlopen(3)ed only when -launchd is given, so Xorg does not link
 it and a server started any other way (startx, xinit, a login window) runs
 exactly as before. Without -launchd nothing changes.
Origin: NextBSD
 (nextbsd/nextbsd-xlibre#13; the XQuartz model: hw/xquartz/mach-startup/launchd_fd.c
 and bundle-main.c, which hand the launchd socket to ListenOnOpenFD)
Forwarded: not yet; a candidate for X11Libre (launchd socket activation outside XQuartz)
 .
 Against 25.1.9: ListenOnOpenFD() (os/connection.c, declared in
 os/client_priv.h) and DisableServerLock() are as in 25.2.2; only the line
 numbers moved.
---
